<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Early Pandyas</h1><p class="st-meta">400 BCE – 300 CE (approx.) · capital Madurai</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Pandya kings of Madurai in the Sangam age, masters of the pearl fisheries of the far south and patrons of early Tamil poetry.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/later-pandyan-empire">Pandyas</a> were one of the three crowned dynasties of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamilakam">Tamilakam</a>, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chera-dynasty">Cheras</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chola-empire">Cholas</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/megasthenes">Megasthenes</a> heard of them around 300 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka's</a> edicts in the 3rd century BCE name the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandyas</a> as independent neighbours beyond his empire. Their heartland was the Vaigai valley around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a> and the coast near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korkai">Korkai</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> wealth came from pearls and conch shells from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mannar">Gulf of Mannar</a>, traded with the Roman world through ports such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korkai">Korkai</a>, and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farming. Tamil tradition places the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sangam-literature">Sangam</a> literary academies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a> under Pandya patronage, and Sangam poems praise kings such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nedunjeliyan-ii">Nedunjeliyan</a>, victor at the battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-talaiyalanganam">Talaiyalanganam</a> over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chera-dynasty">Cheras</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chola-empire">Cholas</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-brahmi">Tamil-Brahmi</a> inscriptions record gifts to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jain</a> monks.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around the 3rd century the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/later-pandyan-empire">Pandyas</a>, like their neighbours, were displaced by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kalabhra-dynasty">Kalabhra dynasty</a>, whose rule is poorly documented. The dynasty revived in the late 6th century as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/first-pandyan-empire">First Pandyan Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kadungon">Kadungon</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/early-pandyas.jpg" alt="Early Pandyas" width="320" height="482"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Venu62 at English Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thanjavur_temple.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>400 BCE – 300 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Madurai</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nedunjeliyan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tamil folk religion, Vedic religion, Jainism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Tamil</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sangam Pandyas</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-50&amp;focus=early-pandyas" aria-label="Early Pandyas on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6834 1807 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Early Pandyas lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7154 2063L7198 2018"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7198 2018h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7154 2063h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7154 2063h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Madurai. <a href="/atlas?year=-50&amp;focus=early-pandyas">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

